  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nStrong gusty winds during the day today. Cannot rule out \nisolated power outages.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nAs the strong low pressure system exits to the NE, a large \nsurface high pressure system will move across the Great Lakes \nand push east. This will cause the pressure gradients to tighten\nthroughout the day today. NNW winds will increase with gusts \nreaching up to 30 mph. Due to the moist soil and leaves on the \ntrees, there is a possibility of isolated tree damage and \nisolated power outages.\n\nAs the high pressure moves into New England, the pressure\ngradients will relax. This will quickly decrease winds this\nevening. Calm winds are expected tonight.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nLocally heavy rainfall possible from Sunday afternoon into\nMonday morning. Minimal impact on drought expected.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nA northern stream trough tracks across southeastern Canada from\nSunday afternoon into late Sunday night, then crosses Maine\nfrom early Monday morning into early Monday evening. At the\nsurface a cold front will approach Sunday, then cross the area\nSunday night and Monday morning. This should bring some \nscattered to possibly numerous showers to the region from late \nSunday afternoon into Monday morning. The strongest forcing will\nbe across the North, with that area in the left rear quadrant \nof a developing 110+ kt upper jet. While surface based CAPE will\nbe minimal, if not non- existent, Showalter indices from late \nSunday afternoon into Sunday evening are around 0, mainly across\nthe North, with mid- level lapse rates of 6.5-7 C/km. So some \nelevated convection, possibly even a rumble of thunder (best \nchance for the latter is across the North, with the best \nforcing) is possible.\n\nPrecipitable water values should range from 1.25 to 1.5 inches \nacross the North and 1.5 to 1.75 inches elsewhere. This will\nallow for locally heavy rainfall with any stronger convection.\nGiven the very dry antecedent conditions, other than possibly\nsome ponding of water on roadways, no significant impacts are\nexpected. Also as of now, this is not expected to have any\nappreciable impact on ongoing drought across the region.",
